rriculum learning strategy during supervised training, allowing the embedding model to progressively learn more nuanced semantic representations.\nL128: Meanwhile, we propose an \\textbf{A}nchor \\textbf{T}oken \\textbf{A}ware (ATA) pooling method that assigns higher weights to anchor tokens based on aggregation patterns observed in LLMs, improving text embedding accuracy without increasing model complexity. Comprehensive experiments on the MTEB benchmark demonstrate that our methods achieve state-of-the-art performance, surpassing existing synthesis strategies both with synthetic data and when combined with public retrieval datasets.\"\nL129:     }\nL130: Download as File\nL131: \nL132:     <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\nL133:     <modsCollection xmlns=\"http://www.loc.gov/mods/v3\">\nL134:     <mods ID=\"pan-etal-2025-negative\">\nL135:         <titleInfo>\nL136:             <title>Negative Matters: Multi-Granularity Hard-Negative Synthesis and Anchor-Token-Aware Pooling for Enhanced Text Embeddings</title>\nL137:         </titleInfo>\nL138:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL139:             <namePart type=\"given\">Tengyu</namePart>\nL140:             <namePart type=\"family\">Pan</namePart>\nL141:             <role>\nL142:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL143:             </role>\nL144:         </name>\nL145:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL146:             <namePart type=\"given\">Zhichao</namePart>\nL147:             <namePart type=\"family\">Duan</namePart>\nL148:             <role>\nL149:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL150:             </role>\nL151:         </name>\nL152:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL153:             <namePart type=\"given\">Zhenyu</namePart>\nL154:             <namePart type=\"family\">Li</namePart>\nL155:             <role>\nL156:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL157:             </role>\nL158:         </name>\nL159:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL160:             <namePart type=\"given\">Bowen</namePart>\nL161:             <namePart type=\"family\">Dong</namePart>\nL162:             <role>\nL163:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL164:             </role>\nL165:         </name>\nL166:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL167:             <namePart type=\"given\">Ning</namePart>\nL168:             <namePart type=\"family\">Liu</namePart>\nL169:             <role>\nL170:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL171:             </role>\nL172:         </name>\nL173:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL174:             <namePart type=\"given\">Xiuxing</namePart>\nL175:             <namePart type=\"family\">Li</namePart>\nL176:             <role>\nL177:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL178:             </role>\nL179:         </name>\nL180:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL181:             <namePart type=\"given\">Jianyong</namePart>\nL182:             <namePart type=\"family\">Wang</namePart>\nL183:             <role>\nL184:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL185:             </role>\nL186:         </name>\nL187:         <originInfo>\nL188:             <dateIssued>2025-07</dateIssued>\nL189:         </originInfo>\nL190:         <typeOfResource>text</typeOfResource>\nL191:         <relatedItem type=\"host\">\nL192:             <titleInfo>\nL193:                 <title>Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)</title>\nL194:             </titleInfo>\nL195:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL196:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Wanxiang</namePart>\nL197:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Che</namePart>\nL198:                 <role>\nL199:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL200:                 </role>\nL201:             </name>\nL202:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL203:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Joyce</namePart>\nL204:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Nabende</namePart>\nL205:                 <role>\nL206:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL207:                 </role>\nL208:             </name>\nL209:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL210:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Ekaterina</namePart>\nL211:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Shutova</namePart>\nL212:                 <role>\nL213:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL214:                 </role>\nL215:             </name>\nL216:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL217:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Mohammad</namePart>\nL218:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Taher</namePart>\nL219:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Pilehvar</namePart>\nL220:                 <role>\nL221:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL222:                 </role>\nL223:             </name>\nL224:             <originInfo>\nL225:                 <publisher>Association for Computational Linguistics</publisher>\nL226:                 <place>\nL227:                     <placeTerm type=\"text\">Vienna, Austria</placeTerm>\nL228:                 </place>\nL229:             </originInfo>\nL230:             <genre authority=\"marcgt\">conference publication</genre>\nL231:             <identifier type=\"isbn\">979-8-89176-251-0</identifier>\nL232:         </relatedItem>\nL233:         <abstract>Text embedding models are essential for various natural language processing tasks, enabling the effective encoding of semantic information into dense vector representations. These models are typically optimized using triplets of (query, positive, negative) data pairs for contrastive learning, where the negative samples play a critical role in enhancing the model’s ability to discern subtle semantic distinctions.\nL234: In this work, we introduce a Multi-Granularity Hard-negative (MGH) synthesis framework that leverages large language models (LLMs) to generate diverse negative samples with varying levels of similarity with the query. This approach facilitates a coarse-to-fine curriculum learning strategy during supervised training, allowing the embedding model to progressively learn more nuanced semantic representations.\nL235: Meanwhile, we propose an Anchor Token Aware (ATA) pooling method that assigns higher weights to anchor tokens based on aggregation patterns observed in LLMs, improving text embedding accuracy without increasing model complexity. Comprehensive experiments on the MTEB benchmark demonstrate that our methods achieve state-of-the-art performance, surpassing existing synthesis strategies both with synthetic data and when combined with public retrieval datasets.</abstract>\nL236:         <identifier type=\"citekey\">pan-etal-2025-negative</identifier>\nL237:         <identifier type=\"doi\">10.18653/v1/2025.acl-long.1501</identifier>\nL238:         <location>\nL239:             <url>https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.1501/</url>\nL240:         </location>\nL241:         <part>\nL242:             <date>2025-07</date>\nL243:             <extent unit=\"page\">\nL244:                 <start>31102</start>\nL245:                 <end>31118</end>\nL246:             </extent>\nL247:         </part>\nL248:     </mods>\nL249:     </modsCollection>\nL250: Download as File\nL251: \nL252:     %0 Conference Proceedings\nL253:     %T Negative Matters: Multi-Granularity Hard-Negative Synthesis and Anchor-Token-Aware Pooling for Enhanced Text Embeddings\nL254:     %A Pan, Tengyu\nL255:     %A Duan, Zhichao\nL256:     %A Li, Zhenyu\nL257:     %A Dong, Bowen\nL258:     %A Liu, Ning\nL259:     %A Li, Xiuxing\nL260:     %A Wang, Jianyong\nL261:     %Y Che, Wanxiang\nL262:     %Y Nabende, Joyce\nL263:     %Y Shutova, Ekaterina\nL264:     %Y Pilehvar, Mohammad Taher\nL265:     %S Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)\nL266:     %D 2025\nL267:     %8 July\nL268:     %I Association for Computational Linguistics\nL269:     %C Vienna, Austria\nL270:     %@ 979-8-89176-251-0\nL271:     %F pan-etal-2025-negative\nL272:     %X Text embedding models are essential for various natural language processing tasks, enabling the effective encoding of semantic information into dense vector representations. These models are typically optimized using triplets of (query, positive, negative) data pairs for contrastive learning, where the negative samples play a critical role in enhancing the model’s ability to discern subtle semantic distinctions.\nL273: In this work, we introduce a Multi-Granularity Hard-negative (MGH) synthesis framework that leverages large language models (LLMs) to generate diverse negative samples with varying levels of similarity with the query. This approach facilitates a coarse-to-fine curriculum learning strategy during supervised training, allowing the embedding model to progressively learn more nuanced semantic representations.\nL274: Meanwhile, we propose an Anchor Token Aware (ATA) pooling method that assigns higher weights to anchor tokens based on aggregation patterns observed in LLMs, improving text embedding accuracy without increasing model complexity. Comprehensive experiments on the MTEB benchmark demonstrate that our methods achieve state-of-the-art performance, surpassing existing synthesis strategies both with synthetic data and when combined with public retrieval datasets.\nL275:     %R 10.18653/v1/2025.acl-long.1501\nL276:     %U https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.1501/\nL277:     %U https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.acl-long.1501\nL278:     %P 31102-31118\nL279: Download as File\nL280: ##### Markdown (Informal)\nL281: \nL282: [Negative Matters: Multi-Granularity Hard-Negative Synthesis and Anchor-Token-Aware Pooling for Enhanced Text Embeddings](https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.1501/) (Pan et al., ACL 2025)\nL283: \nL284:   * cite37†Negative Matters: Multi-Granularity Hard-Negative Synthesis and Anchor-Token-Aware Pooling for Enhanced Text Embeddings (Pan et al., ACL 2025)\nL285: ##### ACL\nL286: \nL287:   * Tengyu Pan, Zhichao Duan, Zhenyu Li, Bowen Dong, Ning Liu, Xiuxing Li, and Jianyong Wang. 2025. cite37†Negative Matters: Multi-Granularity Hard-Negative Synthesis and Anchor-Token-Aware Pooling for Enhanced Text Embeddings . In Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), pages 31102–31118, Vienna, Austria. Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL288: ACL materials are Copyright © 1963–2026 ACL; other materials are copyrighted by their respective copyright holders. Materials prior to 2016 here are licensed under the cite39†Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 3.0 International License†creativecommons.org . Permission is granted to make copies for the purposes of teaching and research. Materials published in or after 2016 are licensed on a cite40†Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License†creativecommons.org .\nL289: The ACL Anthology is managed and built by the cite5†ACL Anthology team of volunteers.\nL290: \nL291: Site last built on 05 October 2026 at 10:49 UTC with cite41†commit aec6a18†github.com .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn29800view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p3573-lin.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p3573-lin.pdf is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2509.01193v1] LobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.01193v1)\nciteturn29800view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.01193v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 1 Sep 2025]\nL9: # Title:LobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Sheng Lin , cite6†Fangcheng Fu , cite7†Haoyang Li , cite8†Hao Ge , cite9†Xuanyu Wang , cite10†Jiawen Niu , cite11†Yaofeng Tu , cite12†Bin Cui L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled LobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data, by Sheng Lin and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:With the breakthrough of Transformer-based pre-trained models, the demand for fine-tuning (FT) to adapt the base pre-trained models to downstream applications continues to grow, so it is essential for service providers to reduce the cost of processing FT requests.\nL17: Low-rank adaption (LoRA) is a widely used FT technique that only trains small-scale adapters and keeps the base model unaltered, conveying the possibility of processing multiple FT tasks by jointly training different LoRA adapters with a shared base model.\nL18: > Nevertheless, through in-depth analysis, we reveal the efficiency of joint FT is dampened by two heterogeneity issues in the training data -- the sequence length variation and skewness. To tackle these issues, we develop LobRA, a brand new framework that supports processing multiple FT tasks by jointly training LoRA adapters. Two innovative designs are introduced.\nL19: Firstly, LobRA deploys the FT replicas (i.e., model replicas for FT) with heterogeneous resource usages and parallel configurations, matching the diverse workloads caused by the sequence length variation. Secondly, for each training step, LobRA takes account of the sequence length skewness and dispatches the training data among the heterogeneous FT replicas to achieve workload balance.\nL20: We conduct experiments to assess the performance of LobRA, validating that it significantly reduces the GPU seconds required for joint FT by 45.03%-60.67%.\nL21: Comments:  | VLDB 2025, version with appendix\nL22: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)\nL23: Cite as:  | cite15†arXiv:2509.01193 [cs.DC]\nL24:    | (or cite16†arXiv:2509.01193v1 [cs.DC] for this version)\nL25:    | cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.01193†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite18†https://doi.org/10.14778/3742728.3742752†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL27: ## Submission history\nL28: \nL29: From: Fangcheng Fu [cite19†view email ]\nL30: [v1] Mon, 1 Sep 2025 07:24:11 UTC (1,012 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled LobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data, by Sheng Lin and 7 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * cite13†View PDF L39:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L40:   * cite20†TeX Source L41: \nL42: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L43: \nL44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: cs.DC\nL47: \nL48: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L49: \nL50: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025-09 L51: \nL52: Change to browse by:\nL53: \nL54: cite27†cs L55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite28†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite29†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com